  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NESA Center Executive Seminar 04-24</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The broader South and Central Asia as well as Southeast Asian region is experiencing rapid and in some cases disruptive - change, ranging from climate change and cyber security threats to continued threats from violent extremist groups and forced migration challenges. With the return of Taliban to power in Afghanistan many extremist groups in the region are feeling empowered and may attempt to find sanctuaries in Afghanistan. The crisis in Myanmar and spillover of refugees in neighboring states, especially Bangladesh is yet another humanitarian crisis that deserves global attention and needed policy intervention. Other perennial tensions between states due to border disputes and interventionist policies of powerful regional players, e.g., China and Russia, are causing additional tensions and distress. These rapidly shifting regional dynamics and security challenges from both traditional and non-traditional threats - also create new opportunities for enhancing regional partnerships and security cooperation to find collaborative solutions to these pressing problems. This seminar will explore both the nature of these challenges and think of creative solutions to build peace, resolve lingering conflicts, and enhance stability in the region.</narrative>
  </description>
